### NTPv5 Status and Interoperability
*   **Draft Status (`draft-ietf-ntp-ntpv5`)**: 
    *   Miroslav Lichvar reported that an updated draft is planned ahead of IETF 121. Recent progress includes resolving the "Flux" issue (the pull request was accepted) and replacing the Bloom filter with a list of NTPv5 node reference IDs to prevent false positives.
    *   Because the updated Bloom filter list only tracks NTPv5 nodes, reference clocks can no longer be observed through it. A new proposed extension for primary reference IDs (tracking sources like GPS or IP addresses) is under consideration. The group needs to decide whether to allow multiple reference IDs via this optional extension or restrict it to primary reference clocks.
    *   Karen O'Donoghue recommended postponing any major document reorganization to keep the draft readable for reviewers. Miroslav Lichvar agreed this can be decided at a later stage.
*   **Interoperability Testing**:
    *   Sarah Grant presented [NTPv5 Interop Status](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-ntp-05/materials/slides-interim-2026-ntp-05-sessa-ntpv5-interop-status-00), detailing testing from the IETF 120 Hackathon in Vienna using `ntp-aparnos`.
    *   Several tracked "failures" in the spreadsheet are actually configuration-related (e.g., version mismatches or local stratum-1 behaviors) rather than protocol errors.
    *   Further implementation work is needed on extension fields (optional vs. mandatory) and standardizing how implementations expose TLS and NTS keys (client-to-server and server-to-client) to facilitate NTS testing.
    *   Karen O'Donoghue supported moving the interoperability tracking resources to the NTP Working Group GitHub repository.

