### Time Synchronization for Deep Space
*   Marc Blanchet presented [Time Sync for Deep Space](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-ntp-05/materials/slides-interim-2026-ntp-05-sessa-time-sync-for-deep-space-00).
    *   Deep space communications (coordinated via the TIPTOP WG) experience delays ranging from seconds (Moon) to tens of minutes (Mars) and rely on store-and-forward buffering.
    *   The proposed draft focuses on providing UTC-based time synchronization to generic payload and surface computers, rather than replacing critical navigation-grade Position, Navigation, and Timing (PNT) systems.
    *   Simulations using Chrony, `ntpdig`, and `ntp.org` yielded a clock error of approximately 100ms. A critical requirement is that propagation delays must be symmetric. 
    *   Traditional NTS is unsuitable due to multi-RTT TCP/TLS handshakes; NTP over QUIC is proposed as a potential alternative.
    *   *Discussion*: Miroslav Lichvar noted that standard NTP clients reject delays exceeding certain thresholds, and the 32-bit fixed-point `root_delay` field in NTP packets has a maximum limit (65,535 seconds in NTPv4, and only 16 seconds in NTPv5). A 24-hour RTT would overflow this field, necessitating an extension field. 
    *   Ruben Nijveld suggested in the chat that the spacecraft or orbiter could act as a new stratum root, eliminating the need to propagate terrestrial root delay. Marc Blanchet agreed this was an excellent point to incorporate into the draft.

