### ITU Liaison and Time Rollovers (2036 / 2038)
*   Karen O'Donoghue reported on the ITU liaison addressing Unix (2038) and NTP (2036) era rollovers.
*   Danny Mayer emphasized that the Unix 2038 rollover must be actively addressed. He argued that systems must explicitly validate timestamp sanity at epoch boundaries, and recommended baking these validation rules into NTPv5.
*   Sarah Grant questioned if other protocols using NTP timestamps (e.g., SSTP) would fail due to a lack of epoch/era indicator fields.
*   Ruben Nijveld noted that because NTPv4 will have a long deployment tail, the working group must address how existing systems handle era jumps, particularly when a local clock boots uninitialized (at zero).
*   Miroslav Lichvar stated that the NTPv4 protocol is technically capable of handling the rollover, making it primarily an implementation issue. He proposed documenting a best practice where implementations pivot incoming timestamps around their software build date.

